Torchlight: RTSS lighting leaves the sum of the lights unclamped, as Runic's
RT Shader System library does (the Xbox build's rtshaderlib/FFPLib_Lighting.hlsl:
FFP_Light_Directional_Diffuse is `vOut = vBaseColour + vDiffuseColour *
saturate(nDotL)`, nothing clamps vOut). Torchlight's lights are brighter than 1:
the town's key light reaches the console's shaders as (1.77, 1.63, 1.15)
(read off a captured town frame). Clamping the sum capped every lit
surface at the texture's own colour. Not __SWITCH__-guarded (a GLSL library
file; both targets need it). The port lights per vertex (FFP_Lighting), as the
console's generated shaders do.

diff --git a/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_PerPixelLighting.glsl b/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_PerPixelLighting.glsl
index 7cca0d5f9..7fc88ffd6 100644
--- a/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_PerPixelLighting.glsl
+++ b/Media/RTShaderLib/SGXLib_PerPixelLighting.glsl
@@ -40,11 +40,11 @@ THE SOFTWARE.
 #include "RTSLib_LTC.glsl"
 #endif
 
-#ifdef USE_LINEAR_COLOURS
+// Torchlight port: Runic's RT Shader System library (the Xbox build's
+// rtshaderlib/FFPLib_Lighting.hlsl, FFP_Light_*_Diffuse) adds each light to the
+// colour and clamps nothing, and its lights are brighter than 1 (the town's key
+// light is 1.77): the sum is left unclamped here too.
 #define FFP_SATURATE(x) x
-#else
-#define FFP_SATURATE(x) saturate(x)
-#endif
 
 #ifdef OGRE_HLSL
 void SGX_Flip_Backface_Normal(in float triArea, in float targetFlipped, inout vec3 normal)
